<div class="page-spacer clearfix">
<div class="content-area" id="primary">
<div class="container">
<div class="row">
<main class="site-main col-xs-12 col-sm-8 left-block" id="main">
<article class="post hentry">
<a href="/en/blog-details.html">
</a>
<header class="entry-header">
</header>
<div class="entry-content">
<h3>Introductory Article</h3>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/vvn1.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: CNN</div>
</div>
<h4>• Introduction</h4>
<p>
    Vovinam is a martial art that embodies the spirit and pride of the Vietnamese people, with a profound influence across the world.<br/>
With a long and storied history, Vovinam has become an indispensable part of Vietnam’s cultural and athletic identity.<br/>
As a locality that has made significant contributions to the preservation, development, and international promotion of this martial art, the Council of Masters and the community of practitioners in Ho Chi Minh City have been honored and recognized for their outstanding dedication.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/vvn2.jpeg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: danviet.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• History and Development</h4>
<p>
    Việt Võ Đạo – Vovinam is one of Vietnam’s major martial arts schools, founded by Grandmaster Nguyễn Lộc in 1938 in Hanoi. Later, it became known simply as Vovinam, which consists of two parts: Vietnamese Martial Arts (Việt Võ Thuật) and Vietnamese Martial Spirit (Việt Võ Đạo).<br>
Although it originated in the North, the growth and flourishing of Vovinam are closely tied to Saigon (now Ho Chi Minh City). In 1960, Grandmaster Lê Sáng succeeded as head of the school, continuing to promote and expand Vovinam. Together with other masters, he worked to systematize the theory, techniques, and philosophy of the art, laying a solid foundation for Vietnamese martial traditions.<br>
In 2010, the Vovinam Masters Council was established, marking a new milestone in the history of the discipline. Under the leadership of Chief Master Nguyễn Văn Chiếu, Vovinam spread widely across the globe. With its national identity, scientific foundation, practicality, and creativity, along with a philosophy of benevolence, honor, and self-sacrifice, Vovinam – Việt Võ Đạo has become a powerful cultural ambassador, introducing the spirit and people of Vietnam to the international community.<br/>
Today, Vovinam has expanded to more than 70 countries and territories, with over 2.5 million practitioners worldwide. It serves as a cultural bridge fostering understanding among nations.<br/>
The establishment of the World Vovinam Federation in 2008 marked a turning point in the art’s global development, followed by the formation of continental federations in Asia, Europe, and Africa. Since then, international competitions such as continental and world championships have been regularly held, with increasing participation and quality. Furthermore, Vovinam has been officially included as a traditional sport in the SEA Games, solidifying its position as a symbol of Vietnamese martial culture on the world stage.
  </br></br></p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/vvn3.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: Vietnamplus.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Technical Features of Vovinam</h4>
<p>
    Based on the foundation of Vietnamese traditional martial arts and wrestling, Vovinam also integrates and refines the essence of other martial arts from around the world. The school absorbs, adapts, and transforms techniques while constantly improving its own foundation according to the principle of the harmony between Hard and Soft (Cương – Nhu phối triển).<br/>
The technical system of Vovinam — including movements, strikes, and training forms — is rich, diverse, and distinctive, reflecting both Vietnamese cultural identity and universal martial values.
  </p>
<h4>• Practicality of Vovinam</h4>
<p>
    This is the most distinctive feature of Vovinam.<br/>
Instead of spending a long period practicing stances and forms before learning self-defense techniques, Vovinam students are immediately instructed by their trainers in countering and escape techniques (such as when being grabbed by the hair, collar, hand, choked, or held around the waist), as well as basic counterattacks (against punches, kicks, or pushes) — all alongside fundamental techniques like blocking, punching, kicking, chopping, and falling — right from the very first training sessions.<br/>
This was a remarkably innovative idea introduced by the late master Nguyễn Lộc in the late 1930s, aiming to help practitioners effectively defend themselves from the start.<br/>
This practicality not only suited the social conditions of that era but remains relevant and valuable today, as martial arts students must balance training with other essential pursuits such as education, professional development, recreation, and making a living.
  </p>
<h4>• Continuity of Techniques</h4>
<p>
    The next distinctive feature is continuity (linked sequences). A single Vovinam technique always consists of at least three actions.<br/>
For example: to counter an opponent’s straight right punch, a student would step the left foot to the left while simultaneously using the right hand to deflect the punch to avoid it; then counterattack by striking the opponent’s eye or face with the left hand, and finish with a low right-hand punch to the opponent’s abdomen. Another strategic combo (continuous attack) — Strategy No. 1 — might include a downward hand chop to the eye or face, followed by a low right-hand punch to the belly, then stepping forward with the right foot to strike the opponent’s temple with the right elbow.<br/>
In general, these can be linked hand techniques (chop, jab, punch, push, elbow…), leg techniques (kick, stomp, sweep, hook…), or combinations of hand and leg strikes (chop + sweep, takedown techniques…). This chained way of striking aims to secure superiority in self-defense and combat, suits the compact and quick build of Vietnamese practitioners, and also provides a backup in case the first one or two moves miss the target.
  </p>
<h4>• Nguyên lý cương nhu phối triển của Vovinam</h4>
<p>
    Hệ thống kỹ thuật Vovinam còn tuân thủ nguyên lý Cương – Nhu phối triển. Lúc bị tấn công, võ sinh thường né tránh (nhu), rồi mới phản công (cương). Bên cạnh đó, Vovinam cũng có nhiều kỹ thuật tấn công nhưng vẫn đảm bảo nguyên lý này; chẳng hạn như khi tung một cú đá tấn công hoặc phản công (cương) vào thân thể đối, võ sinh phải dùng tay che mặt và bảo vệ hạ bộ để thủ (nhu). Ngay trong phương pháp luyện tập té ngã (không nguy hiểm, không đau), võ sinh phải lên gân và co tròn thân người lại (cương), sau đó lăn tròn thân người lúc ngã xuống (nhu) để hóa giải lực tấn công của đối phương và sức rơi của trọng lượng cơ thể. Nhờ vậy, võ sinh Vovinam tập luyện đòn thế và té ngã trên sàn gạch bình thường như trên thảm.
    Nói khác đi, hệ thống kỹ thuật Vovinam bao gồm những thế nhu nhuyễn, các đòn cương mãnh và ngay trong bản thân từng đòn thế cũng chứa đựng sự kết hợp giữa cương – nhu, giống như sự giao hòa giữa âm – dương trong thiên nhiên và xã hội. Cương Nhu phối triển không đơn thuần là sự bao hàm cả 2 tính cương và nhu mà nó linh động, biến hóa. Có lúc cương nhiều, nhu ít; có khi cương ít nhu nhiều; có lúc nửa cương nửa nhu, tùy theo từng hoàn cảnh cụ thể. Nguyên lý này còn thể hiện trong đời sống tinh thần và cách hành xử của võ sinh Vovinam vì:”Cương tượng trưng sự hào hùng, ý chí sắt thép, lòng cương quyết và đức Dũng của con nhà võ. Nhu biểu tượng tính nhu hòa, điềm đạm và lòng Nhân của người võ sĩ. Có cương mà thiếu nhu sẽ không biến hóa, linh hoạt theo từng hoàn cảnh cụ thể. Ngược lại, có nhu nhưng thiếu cương sẽ không phát huy được hiệu quả tối đa”.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/vvn4.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: Vietnamplus.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Applying Scientific Principles</h4>
<p>
    Like other martial arts, Vovinam applies scientific principles to its techniques, such as centrifugal force (in spinning moves, deflections, parries, punches, kicks, and elbow strikes that follow arc or circular paths); trapping/lever force (in locks, joint breaks, hooks, and blocks); rotational force/torque (in straight punches and rotational strikes); and flexion force and explosive power (in throws, slams, grappling, and jumps) — all intended to help practitioners use less energy while maintaining high effectiveness.<br>
In particular, techniques such as sweeping chops, disabling chops, reverse-direction elbow strikes (hand and foot forces delivered simultaneously but in opposite directions), takedown strikes (hand and leg forces delivered simultaneously and in the same direction), and various neck-grappling moves (deployed suddenly when an opponent is off-balance or careless) within the basic kicking and legwork repertoire are important technical hallmarks of Vovinam and are commonly used to bring opponents down.
  </br></p>
<h4>• The Principle of “One Becomes Three”</h4>
<p>
    Another notable aspect of Vovinam is its system of solo, paired, and group training routines. Solo forms (with or without weapons), paired drills (two practitioners performing a sequence of prearranged unarmed or armed techniques), and group exercises (three or four practitioners executing continuous movements according to a set pattern) are designed as logical combinations of basic defense and counterattack techniques. These help practitioners review and reinforce their skills effectively. This embodies the Vovinam principle of “one becomes three”, meaning that from a single technique, multiple variations and applications can develop.<br>
Over the past decade, Vovinam has also introduced Nhu Khí Công Quyền (Soft Energy Forms) for all practitioners, as well as Continuous Paired Forms for older practitioners that emphasize gentle movements without falls or throws. Constantly refined over the past 40 years, Vovinam’s repertoire — including unarmed techniques and weapon forms (knife, sword, staff, hammer, machete, ruler, saber, great saber, etc.) — preserves the martial art’s original characteristics while combining Vietnamese tradition with modern practicality.
  </br></p>
</div>
</article>
